  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What happens at the anode?",
      "a": "A thin layer of platinum catalyst helps each hydrogen molecule split into two protons and two electrons. The protons pass through the membrane, while the electrons are sent round the outside circuit.",
      "asks": [
        "How does a fuel cell split hydrogen?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the membrane do in a fuel cell?",
      "a": "It is a thin plastic sheet that lets protons pass but blocks electrons and gases. That forces the electrons to take the long way round through the wire, which is the current we use.",
      "asks": [
        "What is a proton exchange membrane?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What comes out of a fuel cell?",
      "a": "Just water and some heat. Hydrogen and oxygen combine to make water, so a fuel cell car drips clean water from its exhaust instead of giving off fumes.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a fuel cell clean?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Where does the hydrogen come from?",
      "a": "Most hydrogen today is made from natural gas, which releases carbon dioxide. Green hydrogen is made by splitting water with electricity from wind or solar power, so the whole chain can be clean.",
      "asks": [
        "How is hydrogen fuel made?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How is a fuel cell different from a battery?",
      "a": "A battery stores its chemicals inside and goes flat when they are used up. A fuel cell keeps working as long as hydrogen and air are supplied, so it is refuelled instead of recharged.",
      "asks": [
        "Is a fuel cell a battery?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "William Grove demonstrated an early fuel cell, his gas battery, in 1842.",
    "Fuel cells made electricity on NASA's Apollo spacecraft, and the astronauts used the water they produced."
  ],
